  {
    "episode_id": 96369,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96369.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "You know, when I got to DC, I realized pretty quickly that it was very, very different than any experience I'd ever had in the legislative process. I had served four years in the South Dakota legislature. And there, if you have an idea, you write a bill. It gets scheduled for a hearing. If it passes, it goes to the floor. In three days, it's going to be voted on and then move to the other house. And, you know, there's a normal, predictable process. That doesn't happen in DC at all. In fact, I could have a bill that I write to fix something and it may never get a hearing. I could draft a bill for 20 years in a row and I'm never guaranteed that there's going to be a public discussion on that policy. The only way I can get a bill heard in a committee is if the chairman decides. And then if it passes committee, it may never get voted on on the House floor. There's no time frame. The decision is made by the majority leader and the majority leader alone. And then once you pass something through the house, obviously there's no requirement that the Senate ever look at the bill. So I think that was the biggest surprise to me and opened my eyes to how really broken it is because you can shut down debate pretty quickly on something that's important to you and your state if you can't get that committee chairman to even hold a hearing on it. Everything in DC moves on relationships. So you have to spend time. I figured out pretty quick that I got to make that chairman care enough about either my state, this bill that they're willing to dedicate precious time in front of their committee to it. So building relationships in DC was very, very different than the experience I had in South Dakota and that surprised me the most. It also showed me how broken it is, which should be fixed. I, every Congress, have been one of the ones that has stood up at our House Rules proposal and asked for a normal process to be put into place, much like the South Dakota legislature, because the way that we do it now allows too many important issues to be ignored.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The legislative process in Washington, D.C., differs significantly from the legislative process in South Dakota.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "In South Dakota, a bill follows a predictable sequence: drafting, scheduling for a hearing, floor vote within three days, and passage to the other house.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Committee hearings in D.C. depend solely on the discretion of the committee chairman.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "In D.C., a bill may never receive a hearing despite being drafted multiple times over decades.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Legislative progress in D.C. depends heavily on personal relationships and influence.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Even after passing committee, a bill in D.C. may never reach the House floor for a vote.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Building relationships with key figures like the committee chairman is essential to getting a bill considered in D.C.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "There is no set timeline for legislative action in D.C.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The majority leader holds unilateral power over whether a bill proceeds to floor vote.",
