    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96369.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "I did build good relationships there, but you have to you have to be vocal. You have to spend a lot of time educating folks that you can contribute. I tried very hard not to waste people's time that every time I was speaking, I was offering value to a conversation and then show that I had a background where I could contribute. I think one of the reasons that I got to take such a big role in tax reform was because I was one of the few members on the committee that had actually started a business from scratch and run it. I was a farmer and a rancher, which they needed that voice at the table. I also was a mom who had kids that were still in the house. And so the family tax credits, child tax credits, I could relate to families out there that were still raising our future generations. And so I think that that diversity in my background and experience, they knew I could contribute to the conversation, that I was going to be somebody that would sit in that final negotiation room and give them a perspective that was much needed. There's members of Congress that have run businesses and been involved in industry, but there's a lot that haven't, too. There's a lot that grew up dreaming of being congressman. That's not necessarily a good thing when it comes to doing big pieces of legislation like tax reform. When it came to being on that conference committee, You know, I went to the speaker said, would you please put me on the conference committee to negotiate tax? He said, no, absolutely not. I will not put you on there. And I said, I said, why is that? And he said, because you care too much about the death tax.",
