    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Clarification Request (Specific)"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 96369,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96369.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 47,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, you know, the process concerned me too because I care a lot about that. One of the reasons I ran for Congress was to deal with our debt and our deficit. What we need to understand is the Congressional Budget Office. When they score these bills, they do it in a static mindset. So they can't look at any kind of reaction to policy. We know when you change tax policy, there's a natural reaction that happens by businesses and individuals. If you lower their tax rates, if they're going to reinvest, if you lower a rate for a small business, will they expand? Will they hire more workers? And CBO is not allowed to look at any of that. All they can do is look at actual revenue, and how that's impacted and cuts to dollars that come into the federal government. Now, we know after three years of holding committee hearings, bringing in economists, looking at past history, looking at tax reform that Reagan did 31 years ago, we know that there's always a reaction to a change in tax policy. And so we went in there looking at what kinds of actions did we need to make in this reform bill to create growth in our economy, to get businesses coming back to the United States. to building here, employing folks so that those folks are now paying taxes into the federal treasury. So that 1.5 trillion is that static look, but we project a growth model that will have us in a surplus situation in just a handful of years to come. And that's exactly what we want to see if you look at the",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker cares deeply about the legislative process.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The Congressional Budget Office scores legislation using a static framework.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The CBO cannot account for behavioral reactions to changes in tax policy.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "One reason the speaker ran for Congress was to address national debt and deficits.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The CBO only evaluates changes based on direct impacts to federal revenue.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Changes in tax policy lead to measurable economic responses from businesses and individuals.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Lowering tax rates for small businesses may encourage expansion and hiring.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "A dynamic growth model projected under the reform would result in a budget surplus within several years.",
        "confidence": 0.88
