    "turn_idx": 63,
    "turn_text": "no, I think the world of this president. I think he, you know, I think he says a lot of stuff, but I'm always watching what he does. And I like what he does. So I like that the policies he's pushing. We would have never got tax reform done if it wasn't for him. So I think he's somebody that we're in a better position because he's in the office. But the other thing is that I don't like corporate dollars coming into our state campaigns either. We can't do that in federal campaigns. And it's for a reason. It's so that I'm not making decisions that benefit the corporations that gave me money to get me elected. How does anybody know if a corporation in South Dakota wrote me a check when I was running for governor? When a state contract comes up for bid, how do they know that maybe because that corporation helped me a lot during my campaign, they don't get a little bit of extra information that helps them compete for that contract and have an advantage over someone else who just shows up to compete for that? So I think that to keep it cleaner and have be much more transparent, and have some integrity in our campaign system, that we shouldn't have corporate dollars involved, that we shouldn't have unlimited donations from PACS. I don't have a problem with PAC dollars because they often are advocating for grassroots organizations or throughout the state, but I think unlimited campaign contributions are extremely concerning because they can transfer 100 grand, 200 grand into a candidate's campaign, and then that amount of influence that they hold over that candidate once they're in office can be very detrimental to the general population.",
